  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.4 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper in x, so overshoot shows there first: learning rates above 0.25 overshoot in x, and above 0.5 the x swings grow (diverge without noise).",
    "Noise is simulated: Gaussian, centred on the true slope, standard deviation 2·sqrt((64−b)/(63·b)) per direction for batch size b. That is 2 at b = 1 and exactly 0 at b = 64.",
    "Orange arrow: the mini-batch step, −learning rate × noisy slope, which is how the ball actually moves. Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at their true length (scale 1).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged once the loss passes 10 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Positions off the bowl are drawn at its edge, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "One update happens every 6 host steps (5 per second at 1× speed). Noise comes from the fixed seed and run number, so Reset replays the same noise, and each new run uses the next noise values in the sequence."
  ]
}